WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.136
I don't take anything seriously and I find it hilarious

00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:07.280
when people try to take what I do very seriously.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.210
Aaron Reynolds is the creator of viral meme accounts like "Swear Trek" and "Effin' Birds"

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.981
which features serene woodcut style renderings of birds paired with profane maxims like

00:00:18.981 --> 00:00:21.736
"Get some f*cking popcorn it's sh*tshow time,"

00:00:21.736 --> 00:00:24.486
and "Someone should shove bees up your ass."

00:00:24.486 --> 00:00:28.830
I don't have to yell at people or punch the wall,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.330
I can make a bird very angry and put it on the internet

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.300
and then feel great about it.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.660
In the lead up to the 2020 election just as Reynolds's Effin' Birds Instagram was exploding,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.870
so was panic over so-called disinformation.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.970
Under pressure from lawmakers, tech platforms crack down on content deemed unacceptable

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.490
by their algorithms and human moderators.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.620
Reynolds is based in Canada, but 80 percent of his audience is American

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.570
and Instagram determined that the Effin' Birds account

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.730
had run afoul of the platform's rules

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.560
aimed at stopping foreign election interference.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.745
I needed to turn on location services, 

00:01:04.745 --> 00:01:08.080
because they were concerned about accounts that had a large reach

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.880
and they wanted to make sure that people knew where those accounts were located.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.610
And I was like, okay they're looking for election misinformation
and stuff like that,

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.170
and it's all images.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.690
They can't tell a meme with election disinformation

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.160
from a picture of a bird and the word "Buttsauce."

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.490
Instagram initially added a location label

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.010
to all of Reynolds's posts indicating his account

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:31.010
was based in Canada and he proceeded as normal.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:36.250
But then the company permanently removed "Effin' Birds" from its American explore page,

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.690
which is a recommended list of posts

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:43.809
Instagram shows to users based on their preferences and the posts popularity site wide.

00:01:43.809 --> 00:01:46.270
Effin' Birds's reach on the platform plummeted.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:52.250
On every post I would see that I would get somewhere between five and 10,000 views from the explore page.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.620
And today I get zero on every post no matter how popular.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.980
So did Instagram abuse its market power

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.970
by limiting Reynolds ability to reach new audiences?

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.866
I'm not owed their spotlight on their platform, that's their platform.

00:02:06.866 --> 00:02:10.350
But I think the hard part of it is having had that pulled

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.890
because I'm a dirty foreigner, kinda sucks.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.450
Reynolds distributes his content

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.100
across multiple popular platforms to mitigate his risk.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.270
The reason that I'm on Instagram in the first place

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.400
is because I was stressed out about something like that happening on Twitter.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.920
The algorithms are going to change.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:34.080
And so if you put all your eggs in the basket of that algorithm, you're screwed.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.340
Reynolds says that quirky outlets like his

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.230
could never have found an audience before social media,

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:43.777
which is what led to the publication of his new book available now in hardcover,

00:02:43.777 --> 00:02:46.880
"Effin' Birds: A Field Guide To Identification."

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.490
Nobody wanted to put me on the radio or television

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:54.060
and nobody wanted to publish a book until it was popular on Twitter.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.620
I'm not retreating to legacy media, I'm just finding another platform

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.140
and that platform is bookstores.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.740
Bookstores have been great, there's this sort of magic of learning

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.810
that if you get in good with like an indie book chain,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.550
they'll sell a ton of your books because they love you.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:14.690
Like the number three seller behind Amazon and Barnes Noble

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:20.140
was a Boston area chain called Newbury Comics.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.170
I was like, who are these guys and can I send them all cakes?

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.220
Reynolds says his experience with Instagram isn't an existential
threat to his meme business,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.853
but he does worry about the potential danger of allowing the government 

00:03:32.853 --> 00:03:36.720
through explicit regulation or through its influence over tech companies

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.810
to constrain online speech.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.238
If there's government involvement, we'll have the same kind of problems,

00:03:41.238 --> 00:03:44.210
they'll just be different, or government-mandated versions of those problems.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.240
I think it's funny that I'm lumped in with threats

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.858
to democracy and I post pictures of birds and swear words.

